#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105525 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "97cd9132-458b-5234-a3d2-22b633acb74c" :knowledge-point/title "Reducing an Augmented Matrix for a Singular 2x2 System to REF and Solving the Resulting System"}] :question/id #uuid "7bfabbd2-9988-5c0e-9c17-3d430b3e7a0f" :question/math-academy-id "e-4699" :question/problem "Consider the augmented matrix $M$ given by\n$\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -3 & 1 \\\\ 4 & -12 & 4\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nUse the standard method of Gaussian elimination to reduce $M$ to row echelon form, and then find the general solution of the linear system represented by the matrix." :question/worked-solution "Reducing $M$ to echelon form, we have\n\n$$\nM\\begin{vmatrix}∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -3 & 1 \\\\ 4 & -12 & 4\\end{bmatrix} & R_{2}\\end{vmatrix}:=R_{2} + (-4)R_{1}; ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThe corresponding system is now\n\n$$\nx - 3y = 1; 0x + 0y = 0\n$$\n\nSince any pair of numbers $x,y$ is a solution of the final equation\n\n$$\n0x+0y = 0\n$$\n\nwe can remove this equation from the system. So, our system now consists of a single equation\n\n$$\nx - 3y = 1\n$$\n\nThe first variable $(x)$ is the basic variable, and the second variable $(y)$ is the free variable. Solving for the basic variable $(x)$ in terms of the free variable $(y)$, we get\n\n$$\nx=1+3y\n$$\n\nand the general solution is\n\n$$\nx = 1 + 3y; y = y\n$$\n\nwhere $y$ is an arbitrary number.\n\nIn vector form, the general solution can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix} = \\begin{bmatrix}1 + 3y \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix} = ⟨1 + 3y, y⟩, y ∈ (- ∞, ∞)\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105526 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "07f2ae4c-ff07-5b99-a7b0-90f460081eef" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Singular 2x2 System of Equations Using Gaussian Elimination"}] :question/id #uuid "681fce57-6be7-5907-b575-8c34b4819ae5" :question/math-academy-id "e-4700" :question/problem "Consider the system of linear equations\n$2x + 5y = 4; 4x + 10y = 0$.\nUse the standard method of Gaussian elimination to reduce the augmented matrix $M$ of the system to row echelon form, and then find the general solution of the linear system." :question/worked-solution "First, we set up the augmented matrix for the system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 5 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 10 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nReducing $M$ to row echelon form, we have\n\n$$\nM\\begin{vmatrix}∼\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 5 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 10 & 0\\end{bmatrix} & R_{1}\\end{vmatrix}:=\\frac{1}{2}R_{1}; ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & \\frac{5}{2} & 2 \\\\ 4 & 10 & 0\\end{bmatrix} \\mid R_{2} \\mid:=R_{2} + (-4)R_{1}; ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & \\frac{5}{2} & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -8\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThe corresponding system is now\n\n$$\nx + \\frac{5}{2}y = 2; 0x + 0y =-8\n$$\n\nHowever, the final equation\n\n$$\n0x+0y=-8\n$$\n\nhas no solution. It simplifies to $0=-8$, which is a false statement.\n\nSo, there is no pair $x,y$ that satisfies both equations in the system. Consequently, the overall system has no solution."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106243 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "07f2ae4c-ff07-5b99-a7b0-90f460081eef" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Singular 2x2 System of Equations Using Gaussian Elimination"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d96b95e9-bac4-5e01-9377-a310949893df" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix} = \\begin{bmatrix}-2 - 6y \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}, y ∈ (- ∞, ∞)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186103968} {:answer/id #uuid "2f5e23ee-7b4d-5a4f-a9fd-ff5dba42d251" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix} = \\begin{bmatrix}-2 + 6y \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}, y ∈ (- ∞, ∞)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186103969} {:answer/id #uuid "4981c07e-e9ca-5ec5-b8d0-07fde08d3d98" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "No solution" :db/id #edb/ref 17592